Ian Stirling wrote:
> I've started early training pets.
> When I kill a monster, I drop ?+, pick up the corpse, and then
> throw it at the pet when it drops something.
> Works for me, and it means you don't have to go hunting for tripe/corpses
> when you come to a shop.

	No, it doesn't work for you, actually, though the effects are
subtle enough that it's not obvious that it's really not working.

	There are two factors involved in pet behaviour... tameness and
apport. Tameness controls the likelihood that the pet will go feral.
Apport is the one that controls the odds of the pet fetching for you.

	Tameness increases whenever the pet eats, by the same amount
regardless of whether you handled the food or not or what the food is.
You'd get exactly the same effect on tameness just by leaving the
corpses where they fall for the pet to eat. Messing about dropping stuff
is totally irrelevant.

	Apport increases when you give the pet treats, by an amount that
varies depending on how quickly you feed it after it drops something by
you. The important word in that sentence is "treats"... that means tripe
or meatballs/sticks/rings/etc. for cats and dogs, apples or carrots for
horses, and bananas for monkeys and apes. It does *not* include monster
corpses. They'll eat those, but they're not considered treats, and have
no effect on apport. Trying to train a pet to steal with corpses is a
complete waste of time.

	Also, apport drops over time, so, for best effect, you want to
train it up while you have the shop handy, not significantly beforehand.

